  "metadata": {
    "kind": "formula",
    "name": "Calculated serum osmolality",
    "aliases": [
      "osmolality",
      "osmolar gap",
      "mannitol monitoring"
    ],
    "summary": "Calculates serum osmolality (2×Na + Glucose/18 + BUN/2.8 [+ Ethanol/4.6]) for osmolal gap and osmotherapy monitoring.",
    "short_name": "Serum Osmolality",
    "time_estimate": "< 30 sec",
    "subspecialties": [
      "Neurocritical Care",
      "Stroke"
    ],
    "clinical_pearls": [
      "An osmolal gap (Measured − Calculated > 10 mOsm/kg) suggests unmeasured osmoles such as mannitol, methanol, ethylene glycol, or propylene glycol.",
      "Serum osmolality (mOsm/kg solvent) is measured by an osmometer, whereas calculated formulas using plasma concentrations yield osmolarity (mOsm/L solution); in dilute plasma they are numerically nearly identical.",
      "Pitfall — Extreme hyperglycemia: In hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) and extreme hyperglycemia, the osmolar gap can be skewed and requires clinical judgment."
    ]
  },
